nd Wroth adapt the form."}, {"id": "97b2b937-2ed1-41bf-821f-2e3688342294", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Voice, gender, and power", "children": [{"id": "5d61714d-6ed3-40b4-ac7b-e2258b779b9f", "type": "concept", "title": "Specific terms and topics:"}, {"id": "453735bc-58f7-4967-8898-7f98080bc699", "type": "concept", "title": "speaker"}, {"id": "877f3170-8450-4a0f-8113-d78f4b1647b3", "type": "concept", "title": "addressee"}, {"id": "0b63312a-aec8-4071-b8a9-da7937085026", "type": "concept", "title": "male gaze"}, {"id": "b35360f3-6430-49e9-8287-766fa766905f", "type": "concept", "title": "female authorship"}, {"id": "9061bcdb-6638-4689-8a6f-7a1693068982", "type": "concept", "title": "patronage"}, {"id": "484c3cc3-2f7d-4ca0-81ea-cdbed69e9da6", "type": "concept", "title": "erotic economy"}, {"id": "6e943cbd-560e-4ba1-bc38-62d5a86a882b", "type": "concept", "title": "consent"}, {"id": "ce7c90a1-f875-410e-b776-406427d40f18", "type": "concept", "title": "self-fashioning"}], "description": "The point is to read lyric desire as social performance rather than private feeling alone."}]}, {"id": "37a66f27-5edb-434d-9a6d-3b2489ef7602", "type": "topic", "title": "Lyric Poetry, Court Culture, and Metaphysical Verse", "description": "This section moves from courtly lyric to metaphysical wit, showing how poems stage persuasion, intimacy, devotion, and intellectual play.", "children": [{"id": "4a02333a-1645-455c-bb78-f9d7ffded2f9", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Courtly lyric and manuscript circulation", "children": [{"id": "32831f4f-1447-427d-a3a2-b8056a614bcb", "type": "concept", "title": "Specific terms and topics:"}, {"id": "39d15869-7a78-4735-9cc3-c3e405317d75", "type": "concept", "title": "Wyatt"}, {"id": "e560a776-dd77-43ca-bf20-eeb995f1faf3", "type": "concept", "title": "Surrey"}, {"id": "79cd2c34-39a7-4af1-ac2b-01be8a205d4c", "type": "concept", "title": "Tottel's Miscellany"}, {"id": "778b1c60-423e-4f09-bb94-a1bb0daff2e0", "type": "concept", "title": "coterie circulation"}, {"id